# How much of the system each stack claims to hold. One row per filing.
#
# A filing is never the system
#   There is one system; a filing holds the layers of it that mattered to whoever filed. Reading a
#   stack as a list of the whole system turns a second document's extra layer into an accusation
#   that the first left something out, when the two are two views of one thing.
#
# The third extent is the one two values would lose
#   `scoped` says somebody established what lies outside and excluded it on purpose. `unbounded`
#   says nobody looked. Shown the same way, a bounded selection cannot be told from an unexamined
#   one: the same distinction the coupling search draws one level down.
#
# `complete` can only be refuted in principle
#   Nothing in this corpus could refute it. What would is another filing holding a layer of the same
#   system that this one lacks, and no document names a system, only a filing. So no query here
#   checks it, rather than one that only looks as though it does.
-- pm:Stack/pm:scope, with its pm:basis.
SELECT ss.filing, ss.extent, ss.basis, ss.absent
FROM pm.stack_scope ss
